---
// Goal: show that one option points the same call at TypeSafe's Jev, another
// provider or a model on your own machine with no code change, then list the
// models ThinkThen knows by name.
import SidePage from '../../layouts/SidePage.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
import { BACKEND_PAGES, BACKENDS_SEE, KEY_VARIABLE } from '../../data/catalog.mjs';
import { run } from '../../data/samples.mjs';
import { backends } from '../../lib/backends-table.mjs';
import { pathHtml } from '../../lib/path.mjs';
import { checkLines } from '../../data/backend-checks.mjs';

const page = BACKEND_PAGES[0];
const plan = run('install/backends', '1-plan');
const same = [['typesafe', '1-one'], ['liquid', '2-one'], ['ollama', '2-one']]
  .map(([slug, name]) => ({ ...run(`install/backends/${slug}`, name), see: BACKENDS_SEE[slug][name] }));
const linked = (name) => BACKEND_PAGES.find((p) => p.slug === (['llamacpp', 'mlx'].includes(name) ? 'local' : name));
const rows = backends().map((b) => ({ ...b, page: linked(b.name), checks: checkLines(b.name) }));
for (const r of rows) {
  if (!r.page) throw new Error(`backends: the table holds ${r.name}, and BACKEND_PAGES has no page for it`);
}
const typesafe = rows.find((r) => r.name === 'typesafe');
---
<SidePage pages={BACKEND_PAGES} page={{ ...page, description: "Point the same call at TypeSafe's Jev, another provider or a model on your own machine with one option and no code change." }} name="Models">
  <h1>Models</h1>
  <p class="lede">Point the same call at TypeSafe's Jev, another provider or a model on your own machine. One option makes the switch. Your code stays the same.</p>
  <p>A backend is a named way to reach a model. Before you rely on a provider, <a href="/models/check/"><code>thinkthen backends check</code></a> sends a few fixed questions and one small call through each of the ten functions, and tells you what works.</p>

  <h2 id="one-option">One option</h2>
  <p>These three runs ask the same question about the same line. Each names a different <code>--backend</code>. The Ollama run also passes <code>--url</code>. The site's <a href="/models/ollama/">Ollama</a> listens on a second port. On Ollama's default port, leave <code>--url</code> out.</p>
  {same.map((r) => <Terminal run={r} caption={r.see} />)}
  <p>A different model can give a different answer. Grade a model on your own records before you rely on it. <a href="/start/tune/">Tune for free</a> shows how.</p>

  <h2 id="saved-answers">Saved answers stay with their model</h2>
  <p>A switch of model or address asks every question again. Both are part of each saved answer's <a href="/start/pay-once/#the-key">question key</a>. The answers of two models never mix.</p>

  <h2 id="named">The models ThinkThen knows by name</h2>
  <p>Name one with <code>--backend NAME</code> or <code>THINKTHEN_BACKEND</code>. Each brings its own address, key variable and model.</p>
  <p><a href="/models/local/">Run a model locally</a> shows Ollama, llama.cpp and the Strands MLX server. Some models also read JPEG and PNG images. The <a href="/start/files/#images">image input rules</a> name them.</p>
  <table class="backends" data-backends>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Address</th><th>Path</th><th>Key variable</th><th>Default model</th></tr>
    {rows.map((r) => (
      <tr data-backend={r.name}>
        <td><a href={r.page.route}><code>{r.name}</code></a></td>
        <td><code class="path" set:html={pathHtml(r.base)} /></td>
        <td><code>{r.path}</code></td>
        <td>{r.keys.map((k, i) => <Fragment>{i > 0 && ', then '}<code>{k}</code></Fragment>)}</td>
        <td><code>{r.model}</code></td>
      </tr>
    ))}

  </table>
  <h2 id="tested">Tested models</h2>
  <p>This table gives the last <a href="/models/check/"><code>thinkthen backends check</code></a> of each built-in backend, with its date. A check covers its own date, model and inputs.</p>
  <table class="backend-checks">
    <tr><th>Backend</th><th>Date</th><th>Model</th><th>Check result</th></tr>
    {rows.flatMap((r) => r.checks.map((c) => (
      <tr>
        <td><a href={r.page.route}><code>{r.name}</code></a></td>
        <td>{c.date}</td>
        <td><code>{c.model}</code></td>
        <td>{c.link ? <a href={c.link}>{c.critical} critical, {c.warning} warning</a> : `${c.critical} critical, ${c.warning} warning`}</td>
      </tr>
    )))}
  </table>
  <p>Any other server goes in through <a href="/models/system-one/">its address or the configuration file</a>. <a href="https://github.com/botassembly/awesome-thinkthen">Awesome ThinkThen</a> lists more models and servers that pass the check.</p>

  <h2>With no backend named</h2>
  <p>A command that names no backend and no address sends every question to <code>{typesafe.base}</code> with <code>{typesafe.model}</code>. It reads the key from <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code>. The <a href="/models/typesafe/">TypeSafe Jev page</a> covers that backend.</p>
  <Terminal run={plan} caption="The plan names the address, the model, and the variable a key would come from. It holds no key." />
  <p><code>--plan</code> sends nothing and needs no key. The plan holds the evidence. Treat it with the same care as the request.</p>

  <h2 id="the-key">The key</h2>
  <p>A named backend reads only its own key variables, and the first one that is not blank wins. With no backend named, the key comes from <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code>. The command takes no flag and no file for a key. A key that is unset or blank exits 4 at any address but the three local hosts named below. The message names the variable and never a value.</p>
  <p>Your key goes only to the address you named. <a href="/start/your-data/#key">Your data stays yours</a> says where it never shows.</p>
  <p>A plain <code>http://</code> address may reach only <code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code> or <code>[::1]</code>. Any other <code>http://</code> host is a usage error. The key would cross the network in clear text. At those three hosts a request goes out with no key when the key variable is unset, whichever backend is named. Under <code>http://</code> the tool ignores every proxy variable.</p>
  <p>An address with a user name, a query, or a fragment is a usage error. The plan prints the address, and a saved answer keeps it.</p>

  <h2>Where your text goes</h2>
  <p>Each backend's own terms govern what it keeps. The default address sends your question and evidence to TypeSafe. Its <a href="https://typesafe.ai/legal/mca">customer agreement</a>, <a href="https://typesafe.ai/legal/data-processing">data processing addendum</a>, and <a href="https://typesafe.ai/legal/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> describe data handling. The privacy policy, checked 2026-09-28, gives no fixed retention period for API input. Check the terms that govern your account before you send sensitive text. The answer cache has its own <a href="/start/settings/#answer-cache">storage setting</a>.</p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/start/configuration/">Configuration</a> · <a href="/start/settings/">Every setting and its default</a> · <a href="/trust/">Test it before you trust it</a></p>
</SidePage>

<style>
  table.backends { width: 100%; table-layout: fixed; }
  table.backends :is(th, td) { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  table.backends code { white-space: normal; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(1) { width: 16%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(2) { width: 18%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(3) { width: 15%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(4) { width: 25%; }
  table.backends th:nth-child(5) { width: 26%; }
</style>
